  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "If \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are real numbers, the equation \\(3x-5+a=bx+1\\) has a unique solution \\(x\\) [the symbol \\(a\\ne0\\) means that \\(a\\) is different from zero]:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "for all \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)",
    "B": "if \\(a\\ne2b\\)",
    "C": "if \\(a\\ne6\\)",
    "D": "if \\(b\\ne0\\)",
    "E": "if \\(b\\ne3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Collecting the terms containing \\(x\\) reveals when we can isolate the unknown:\n\\[\n3x-5+a=bx+1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(3-b)x=6-a.\n\\]\nIf \\(b\\ne3\\), the coefficient \\(3-b\\) is nonzero, so division gives exactly one solution,\n\\[\nx=\\frac{6-a}{3-b}.\n\\]\nIf \\(b=3\\), we must not divide by \\(3-b\\). Instead, the equation is \\(0=6-a\\). When \\(a=6\\), every real \\(x\\) is a solution; when \\(a\\ne6\\), there is no solution.\n\nTherefore the equation has a unique solution exactly when \\(\\boxed{b\\ne3}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
